{
  "subtype": "faceted_decomposition",
  "concept": "incident_delay_reasons",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "open_to_close_days_bucket",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "restatement",
      "rationale": "This is the resolution-duration outcome itself (how long incidents took), i.e. the very thing to be explained, not a constitutive aspect of the REASONS for delay."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "The type of incident (email, database, vpn) is a legitimate aspect of characterizing why classes of incidents differ in delay; it is a facet of the delay-reason taxonomy (nature of the problem)."
    },
    {
      "name": "failure_mode",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "The kind of failure (outage, authentication_failure, crash) is a constitutive aspect of what goes wrong and thus of the reason an incident is hard/slow to resolve."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_component",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Which component is affected (server_side, network_infrastructure, client_application) is an aspect of the problem's nature, a facet of the delay-reason characterization."
    },
    {
      "name": "scope_indicator",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Scope/blast radius (system_wide, individual_user) is a constitutive aspect of an incident's difficulty and a plausible facet of delay reasons."
    },
    {
      "name": "assigned_at_open",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "external_predictor",
      "rationale": "Whether assignment happened at open is a process/workflow attribute that may predict delay but is not itself an aspect OF the delay reason concept."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolver_match",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "external_predictor",
      "rationale": "Whether resolver matched is an operational/routing predictor of delay, not a constitutive part of the delay-reason concept."
    },
    {
      "name": "assignee_id",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "external_predictor",
      "rationale": "The identity of the assignee is an external actor attribute that may correlate with delay but is not a facet of the reason itself."
    },
    {
      "name": "caller_id_group",
      "is_constitutive_facet": false,
      "role": "external_predictor",
      "rationale": "Caller identity is an external entity attribute, not a constitutive aspect of why resolution is delayed."
    }
  ],
  "facet_columns": ["incident_category", "failure_mode", "affected_component", "scope_indicator"],
  "non_facet_columns": ["open_to_close_days_bucket", "assigned_at_open", "resolver_match", "assignee_id", "caller_id_group"],
  "mutual_exclusivity": 0.5,
  "exhaustiveness": 0.55,
  "rationale": "Only four of nine columns are genuine facets of the delay-reason concept: incident_category, failure_mode, affected_component, and scope_indicator all describe the intrinsic nature of the problem. The remaining columns fail the meronymy test: open_to_close_days_bucket restates the outcome (duration) being explained; assigned_at_open, resolver_match, assignee_id, and caller_id_group are external process/actor predictors, not constitutive parts of the reason. Among the true facets, mutual exclusivity is weakened because incident_category, failure_mode, and affected_component substantially overlap (e.g., 'vpn'/'network_connectivity' category, 'connection_failure' mode, and 'network_infrastructure' component often encode the same underlying situation), so they do not cleanly carve non-overlapping aspects. Exhaustiveness is moderate: the facets cover problem-type, failure-kind, component, and scope, but omit key delay-driving aspects such as diagnostic complexity, dependency/vendor involvement, information/resource availability, and required remediation effort."
}

